A_1 \xrightarrow{\quad a \quad} A_0, \quad
B_1 \xrightarrow{\quad b \quad} B_0, \quad
C_1 \xrightarrow{\quad c \quad} C_0,
\end{aligned}\] where  $A_i, B_i, C_i = \mathbb{F}_2[G]$  and the boundary maps  $a, b, c \in \mathbb{F}_2[G]$ . The tensor (balanced) product complex is formed from these three complexes using the  isomorphism $\mathbb{F}_2[G] \otimes_G \mathbb{F}_2[G] \simeq \mathbb{F}_2[G]$  which is used to simplify the general definition. The resulting tensor product complex is: \[\begin{aligned}
A_1 \otimes_G B_1 \otimes_G C_1
\xrightarrow{\quad \partial_3 \quad}
\bigoplus_{i+j+k=2} A_i \otimes_G B_j \otimes_G C_k
\xrightarrow{\quad \partial_2 \quad}
\bigoplus_{i+j+k=1} A_i \otimes_G B_j \otimes_G C_k
\xrightarrow{\quad \partial_1 \quad} A_0 \otimes_G B_0 \otimes_G C_0
\end{aligned}\] The boundary maps can be written explicitly in terms of a, b, and c as: \[\begin{aligned}
\partial_3 &= \begin{bmatrix} a \\ b \\ c \end{bmatrix} \\
\partial_2 &= \begin{bmatrix}
0 & c & b \\
c & 0 & a \\
b & a & 0
\end{bmatrix} \\
\partial_1 &= \begin{bmatrix} a & b & c \end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}\] The trivariate tricycle codes from this complex are constructed in ( Jacob  et al. , 2025 ) by defining the group  $G = \Z_\ell \times \Z_m \times \Z_p$  for integers  $\ell$ , m, and p, which is  isomorphic  to the group generated by the following matrices: \[\begin{aligned}
x &= S_\ell \otimes I_m \otimes I_p \\
y &= I_\ell \otimes S_m \otimes I_p \\
z &= I_\ell \otimes I_m \otimes S_p
\end{aligned}\] where  $S_n$  is the  $n \times n$  cyclic shift matrix with elements  $(S_n)_{i,j} = \delta_{i, j \oplus 1}$  and  $\oplus$  denotes addition modulo n. ( Jacob  et al. , 2025 ) considers trivariate polynomials A, B, and C in the non-commuting variables x, y, and z. Their initial, symmetric choice is: \[\begin{aligned}
A &= A_1 + A_2 + A_3 \\
B &= B_1 + B_2 + B_3 \\
C &= C_1 + C_2 + C_3
\end{aligned}\] though this restriction can be relaxed. Parity Checks The parity-check matrices for the CSS code as: \[\begin{aligned}
H_X \equiv \partial_1, \quad H_Z = \partial_2^\top, \quad M_Z = \partial_3^\top,
\end{aligned}\] where  $H_X$  and  $H_Z$  are the  $X$  and  $Z$  stabilizer generators, and  $M_Z$  is a matrix of meta-checks encoding the redundancy in the  $Z$  checks. It can be verified that: \[\begin{aligned}
H_XH_Z^\top = 0 \quad \text{and} \quad M_ZH_Z = 0.
\end{aligned}\] This results in the chain complex structure: \[\begin{aligned}
C_M \xrightarrow{\quad M_Z \quad} C_Z \xrightarrow{\quad H_Z \quad} Q \xrightarrow{\quad H_X \quad} C_X
\end{aligned}\] where the vector spaces over  $\mathbb{F}_2[G]$ . The matrices are given by: \[\begin{aligned}
M_Z &= \begin{bmatrix} A^\top & B^\top & C^\top \end{bmatrix} \\
H_Z &= \begin{bmatrix}
0 & C^\top & B^\top \\
C^\top & 0 & A^\top \\
B^\top & A^\top & 0
\end{bmatrix} \\
H_X &= \begin{bmatrix} A & B & C \end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}\] Polynomial Quotient Ring Formalism Note We leverage the isomorphism between the group algebra  $\mathbb{F}_2[\mathbb{Z}_\ell \times \mathbb{Z}_m \times \mathbb{Z}_p]$  and the multivariate polynomial quotient ring  $\frac{\mathbb{F}_2[x, y, z]}{\langle x^\ell-1, y^m-1, z^p-1 \rangle}$  to introduce a novel realization of the Trivariate Tricycle codes of ( Jacob  et al. , 2025 ). Generalization The generalization of the  TrivariateTricycle  codes to an arbitrary number of groups (Λ ≥ 3) remains a conjecture; for details, see Section 5.3.2,  Product of Λ ≥ 3 group algebra codes  of ( Breuckmann  et al. , 2024 ). The parity-check and metacheck matrices for the  TrivariateTricycle  codes—which are equivalent to the boundary maps of the underlying 3-term chain complex, see equation 2, 3, and 4 of ( Jacob  et al. , 2025 )—are constructed explicitly in ( Breuckmann  et al. , 2024 ). These matrices serve as the fundamental building blocks and provide the key theoretical insight underlying this family of quantum codes. Examples Here is the  [[72, 6, 6]]  trivariate tricycle code from Table I from ( Jacob  et al. , 2025 ). julia> using Oscar; using QuantumClifford.ECC;

julia> l, m, p = 4, 3, 2;

julia> R, (x, y, z) = polynomial_ring(GF(2), [:x, :y, :z]);

julia> I = ideal(R, [x^l - 1, y^m - 1, z^p - 1]);

julia> S, _ = quo(R, I);

julia> A = S(1 + y + x*y^2);

julia> B = S(1 + y*z + x^2*y^2);

julia> C = S(1 + x*y^2*z + x^2*y);

julia> c = TrivariateTricycle(l, m, p, A, B, C);

julia> code_n(c), code_k(c)
(72, 6) Here is the  [[432, 12, 12]]  trivariate tricycle code from Table I from ( Jacob  et al. , 2025 ). julia> using Oscar; using QuantumClifford.ECC;

julia> l, m, p = 6, 6, 4;

julia> R, (x, y, z) = polynomial_ring(GF(2), [:x, :y, :z]);

julia> I = ideal(R, [x^l - 1, y^m - 1, z^p - 1]);

julia> S, _ = quo(R, I);

julia> A = S(1 + x*y*z^3 + x^3*y^4*z^2);

julia> B = S(1 + x^3*y*z^2 + x^3*y^2*z^3);

julia> C = S(1 + x^4*y^3*z^3 + x^5*z^2);

julia> c = TrivariateTricycle(l, m, p, A, B, C);

julia> code_n(c), code_k(c)
(432, 12) Fields l::Int64 : Order of the first abelian group in  $\mathbb{F}_2[\mathbb{Z}_\ell \times \mathbb{Z}_m \times \mathbb{Z}_p]$ m::Int64 : Order of the second abelian group in  $\mathbb{F}_2[\mathbb{Z}_\ell \times \mathbb{Z}_m \times \mathbb{Z}_p]$ p::Int64 : Order of the third abelian group in  $\mathbb{F}_2[\mathbb{Z}_\ell \times \mathbb{Z}_m \times \mathbb{Z}_p]$ A::Oscar.MPolyQuoRingElem{Nemo.FqMPolyRingElem} : First trivariate polynomial in quotient ring  $\frac{\mathbb{F}_2[x, y, z]}{\langle x^\ell-1, y^m-1, z^p-1 \rangle}$ B::Oscar.MPolyQuoRingElem{Nemo.FqMPolyRingElem} : Second trivariate polynomial in quotient ring  $\frac{\mathbb{F}_2[x, y, z]}{\langle x^\ell-1, y^m-1, z^p-1 \rangle}$ C::Oscar.MPolyQuoRingElem{Nemo.FqMPolyRingElem} : Third trivariate polynomial in quotient ring  $\frac{\mathbb{F}_2[x, y, z]}{\langle x^\ell-1, y^m-1, z^p-1 \rangle}$ source QuantumClifford.ECC.boundary_maps  —  Method Returns all boundary maps of the chain complex, including both  parity check  and  metacheck  matrices. Here are the boundary maps of  [[12, 1, 2]] 3D  Surface code with  L = 2  from ( Berthusen  et al. , 2024 ). julia> using Oscar; using QuantumClifford; using QECCore;

